Evaluate $b_{i,k}(\lambda)=(q^{\lambda_i-i}-q^{\lambda_{k+1}-k})/(q-1)$ and $c_{i,k}(\lambda)=(q^{\lambda_i-i}-q^{\lambda_k-k})/(q-1)$. Let $B_{i,j}$ be the zero-$b$ set in $[i,j)$ and $C_{i,j}$ the zero-$c$ set in $(i,j)$. Their vanishing is exactly the corresponding equal-residue comparison. If $c_{i,k+1}=0$, then $b_{i,k}
e0$, because $c_{i,k+1}-b_{i,k}=q^{\lambda_{k+1}-k-1}$ is a unit. Write $B\downarrow C$ for an injection of demands $B$ into supports $C$, sending each demand to a weakly smaller support. For $C\subseteq C_{i,j}$, we claim nonzero $S_{i,j}((i,j)\setminus C)z$ in $L$ implies $B_{i,j}\downarrow C$. Induct on $j-i$. At length one the elementary commutator and positive detection give equivalence with $b_{i,i}
e0$. At larger length some $E_l$ has nonzero image: otherwise all positive divided powers kill this vector, since its deficit has each simple-root coefficient zero or one, so powers greater than one have absent target weights; triangular spanning would generate a proper second highest submodule of the simple $L$. Use the table of step 3.1. In the split case its disjoint factors commute, so both their vectors are nonzero; induction gives matchings on both intervals, and $c_{i,l+1}=0$, $b_{i,l}
e0$ identify the split demands with the full ones. If $j-1\in C$, induction on the shortened interval matches old demands and the extra demand, when present, uses $j-1$. Otherwise put $h=\max(\{i\}\cup C)$ in the last table row. A nonzero first term has $b_{h,j-1}=b_{i,j-1}
e0$, so there is no new demand. A nonzero second term matches old demands into $C\setminus\{h\}$; the released largest support $h$ handles the only possible new demand $j-1$. This proves the claim in every case.
